ing the king and winning the bishop back. However, in this specific board state, the square c2 is occupied by a White pawn, preventing the White Queen from reaching a4.\n2.  **Trapping threat**: If White plays c3 or a3 after the capture, the bishop has safe retreat squares (e.g., a5, e7, c5), so it is not trapped.\n3.  **Other moves**: Developing moves like N: g8-f6 or N: b8-c6 are solid, but they do not capitalize on the tactical error of leaving b4 hanging. B: c8-h3 would trade bishops, but simply winning a pawn is generally better than an equal trade.\n\nReason: Capturing the pawn on b4 yields a clean material advantage (Black goes up one pawn) and develops the King-side bishop active into the game. The move is tactically sound because the standard refutations (like the Queen fork) are physically blocked on the current board.\n\nAnswer: B: f8-b4"}
